l was the first European to visit and cross the river in August 1836, noting its width and its suitability for boating.\n</p>\nThe Glenelg River was also important to Indigenous Australians. It formed the traditional tribal boundaries for the nomadic Bungandidj people (western bank) and Gunditjmara people (eastern bank) people from two distinct language groups.</div>", "source": "commons-desc-page"}, "DateTimeOriginal": {"value": "circa 1905<div style=\"display: none;\">date QS:P571,+1905-00-00T00:00:00Z/9,P1480,Q5727902</div>", "source": "commons-desc-page"}, "Credit": {"value": "The History Trust of South Australian,  South Australian Government <br> Photo <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external autonumber\" href=\"http://data.history.sa.gov.au/sagpc/media/preview/5975?maxsize=1000\">[1]</a> <br> Object record <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external autonumber\" href=\"http://data.history.sa.gov.au/sagpc/getitem/5975\">[2]</a>", "source": "commons-desc-page"}, "Permission": {"value": "<table style=\"width:100%; clear:both; text-align:center; margin:0.5em auto; background-color:var(--background-color-neutral-subtle, #f8f9fa); border: 2px solid var(--border-color-subtle, #c8ccd1); padding: 0; border-spacing: 8px; color:var(--color-base, #202122);\" dir=\"ltr\" class=\"layouttemplate licensetpl\" lang=\"en\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"width:90px;\" rowspan=\"2\"><span class=\"skin-invert\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><span title=\"Creative Commons\"><img alt=\"Creative Commons\" src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Cc.logo.circle.svg/40px-Cc.logo.circle.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"28\" height=\"28\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Cc.logo.circle.svg/60px-Cc.logo.circle.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x\" data-file-width=\"64\" data